import{_ as a,o as t,c as r,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.Bx6eOp9J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"2026-10-27 Agent Research and Product Intelligence","description":"CatchBench consumes auditable, adaptive gateway evaluation, pre-dispatch review, idempotent recovery and competitor delivery changes.","frontmatter":{"title":"2026-10-27 Agent Research and Product Intelligence","description":"CatchBench consumes auditable, adaptive gateway evaluation, pre-dispatch review, idempotent recovery and competitor delivery changes."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-27.md","filePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-27.md","lastUpdated":1793106296000}'),i={name:"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-27.md"};function s(o,e,l,c,d,p){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="_2026-10-27-agent-research-and-product-intelligence" tabindex="-1">2026-10-27 Agent Research and Product Intelligence <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-10-27-agent-research-and-product-intelligence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2026-10-27 Agent Research and Product Intelligence&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The central developments are <strong>audit evidence that proves the real call path, recovery based on durable identity and ownership, and authorization bound to final execution arguments</strong>. Zhao-linked CatchBench and AEGIS strengthen library-consumption and realized-attack evidence. Paperclip and Eigent repair duplicate execution and incomplete proofs. Superset becomes Paradev and releases desktop 2.0.0.</p><p>Run start: 2026-10-27 20:50:12 Asia/Shanghai. Main incremental window: October 26 10:01:41 to this run start, deduplicated against the last successful checks and published October 26 report. Incomplete sources retain earlier pending windows. Older material is not presented as new. Every CodeFlowMu/FCoP relationship below is a <strong>pending validation lead</strong>, not a development assignment or completed experiment.</p><h2 id="_1-yue-zhao-catchbench-0-1-3-consumes-auditable-baselines" tabindex="-1">1. Yue Zhao: CatchBench 0.1.3 consumes auditable baselines <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-yue-zhao-catchbench-0-1-3-consumes-auditable-baselines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Yue Zhao: CatchBench 0.1.3 consumes auditable baselines&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Released on October 26 at 03:01 UTC. Seventy-nine board entries now obtain scores through auditable &gt;=0.3.0. LLM-judge cache replay checks the library-generated prompts, parsed responses and recorded driver decisions and raises on mismatches. Consumption tests disable original scoring cores and perturb library outputs to establish that the boards use the library. Data, labels, splits, protocols and metrics are unchanged; board values are identical to 0.1.2. This is an implementation/reproducibility change, not a performance gain.</p><p>Pending CodeFlowMu/FCoP lead: verify that benchmark scoring actually consumes the production method library. Interaction topic: coverage across pre-run, live-prefix and post-run audit states, and why some baselines intentionally remain original.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/catchbench/releases/tag/v0.1.3" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://pypi.org/project/catchbench/0.1.3/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_2-zhao-linked-aegis-tests-the-gateway-with-an-agent-that-follows-injections" tabindex="-1">2. Zhao-linked AEGIS tests the gateway with an agent that follows injections <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-zhao-linked-aegis-tests-the-gateway-with-an-agent-that-follows-injections" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Zhao-linked AEGIS tests the gateway with an agent that follows injections&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：COMMITTED; UPSTREAM RESULTS, NOT LOCALLY REPRODUCED</strong></p><p>Commit 9cc08d1 adds adaptive AgentDojo experiments and artifacts. In an earlier Haiku 4.5 experiment the agent never realized an attack, which could not establish gateway protection. A local qwen3-8b agent now realizes 61 of 249 undefended banking/Slack attack pairs; those 61 become targets for four rewrite rounds. Verdict-only and white-box feedback each flag/hold 60/61 (98.4%), with 1/61 escaping. Escape requires both AgentDojo security success and an allowed sink tool. These are upstream results, not locally reproduced or a universal safety rate.</p><p>The README identifies Aojie Yuan, Zhiyuan Su and Yue Zhao and links USC FORTIS; this attribution does not extend to unrelated projects named Aegis. Pending lead: separate model refusal from gateway enforcement. Interaction: the escaping path, benign multi-step hold costs and final approval semantics.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/Justin0504/Aegis/commit/9cc08d10f31bf6ba2511a526db2ee14324863924" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Justin0504/Aegis/blob/9cc08d10f31bf6ba2511a526db2ee14324863924/research/paper-arxiv/figures/table_adaptive_agentdojo.tex" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_3-codex-adds-pre-dispatch-guardian-review-and-cross-process-session-locking" tabindex="-1">3. Codex adds pre-dispatch Guardian review and cross-process session locking <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-codex-adds-pre-dispatch-guardian-review-and-cross-process-session-locking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Codex adds pre-dispatch Guardian review and cross-process session locking&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; STABLE RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#57433 reviews opted-in native function calls synchronously before dispatch and prevents rejected handlers from executing. Hook-rewritten arguments are reviewed instead of the original encrypted replay content. #57441 registers native multi-agent history as an under-development, disabled-by-default flag. #57358 coordinates session_index.jsonl mutations across processes with a persistent lock; 30-second contention times out without writes, and caller cancellation does not release a still-working worker&#39;s lock. The latest stable release remains 0.164.0; new alpha releases do not establish stable availability.</p><p>Pending lead: bind call-time authorization to the final executed arguments and retain write ownership through cancellation. Interaction: rewritten arguments, review retries, evidence-budget truncation and missing multi-agent history.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/57433" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/57441" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/57358" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.164.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 4</a></p><h2 id="_4-openai-agents-python-adds-opt-in-handoff-recovery-without-rollback" tabindex="-1">4. OpenAI Agents Python adds opt-in handoff recovery without rollback <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-openai-agents-python-adds-opt-in-handoff-recovery-without-rollback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. OpenAI Agents Python adds opt-in handoff recovery without rollback&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#5418 adds optional failure_error_function feedback for handoff argument correction; None preserves exceptions. Handled failures stay on the caller without successful-transfer hooks/history filters. Callback errors may be recovered without rolling back side effects; cancellation and transfer hook/filter failures still propagate. In #5430, seratch preserves nested record boundaries by splitting only on the writer&#39;s LF delimiter, rather than Unicode/control separators. #5431 excludes vulnerable MCP dependencies, including affected 2.0/2.1, while supporting patched 1.x/2.x.</p><p>Pending lead: validate protocol recovery, side-effect idempotency and message-role boundaries separately. Interaction: which post-side-effect callback errors must be re-raised instead of retried. Seratch directly contributes these core fixes.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/pull/5418" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/pull/5430" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/pull/5431" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_5-claude-code-and-copilot-cli-ship-lifecycle-and-long-task-fixes" tabindex="-1">5. Claude Code and Copilot CLI ship lifecycle and long-task fixes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-claude-code-and-copilot-cli-ship-lifecycle-and-long-task-fixes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Claude Code and Copilot CLI ship lifecycle and long-task fixes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Claude Code 2.1.311 fixes repeated SubagentStop hooks during execution, deletion of an isolated worktree while its background child still works, unavailable late-connecting MCP tools in subagents, and rerunning a completed subagent skill after restart. It adds idle-hold options for self-hosted-runner. Copilot CLI 1.0.104 keeps interactive/SDK Autopilot waiting for attached background tasks, while -p --autopilot retains its wait timeout, and makes Assisted Permissions available without experimental mode.</p><p>Pending lead: tie hooks, workspace reclamation and waiting to the actual child-task lifecycle. Interaction: distinguish legitimate unattended waits from unbounded hangs and expose evidence that children remain active.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/releases/tag/v2.1.311" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/github/copilot-cli/releases/tag/v1.0.104" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_6-langgraph-1-2-18-ships-recovery-final-checkpoints-and-snapshot-tradeoffs" tabindex="-1">6. LangGraph 1.2.18 ships recovery, final checkpoints and snapshot tradeoffs <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-langgraph-1-2-18-ships-recovery-final-checkpoints-and-snapshot-tradeoffs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. LangGraph 1.2.18 ships recovery, final checkpoints and snapshot tradeoffs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 1.2.18 ships error_handler recovery across paths, failed-task results in streams and the final checkpoint when interrupt_after or a recursion limit stops execution. DeltaChannel now snapshots every 100 updates rather than 1,000, trading more storage for faster long-thread reads; snapshot_frequency=1000 restores the old cadence. SDK 0.4.10 fixes runs.wait retries and SSE handling; checkpoint 4.3.1 rejects NUL in namespace labels.</p><p>Pending lead: align visible terminal state, error events and recovery checkpoints, then measure snapshot costs on real long tasks. Interaction: deduplication and client cursors across failure/recovery streams.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/releases/tag/1.2.18" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/releases/tag/sdk%3D%3D0.4.10" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/releases/tag/checkpoint%3D%3D4.3.1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_7-mcp-apps-simplifies-example-approvals-webmcp-bridge-remains-a-merge" tabindex="-1">7. MCP Apps simplifies example approvals; WebMCP bridge remains a merge <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-mcp-apps-simplifies-example-approvals-webmcp-bridge-remains-a-merge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. MCP Apps simplifies example approvals; WebMCP bridge remains a merge&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：2.0.4 RELEASED; #829/#835 MERGED</strong></p><p>Released 2.0.4 leaves the library API unchanged. Its pdf-server example consolidates 13 action tools into interact, retaining --split-app-tools. Batches return position-aligned results and ERROR slots rather than an aggregate isError. Later #829 merges bidirectional App/WebMCP bridging and deduplication rules, but #835 reverts the 2.1.0 version bump. Bridging must not be presented as shipped in 2.0.4 or as a verified 2.1.0 release.</p><p>Pending lead: reduce approval noise without losing action-level authorization; consume one discovery surface per view. Interaction: partial batch failures, permission granularity and prevention of duplicate execution across bridges.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/releases/tag/v2.0.4" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/pull/829" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/pull/835" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_8-paperclip-fixes-recovery-ownership-completeness-windows-saves-and-pwa-installability" tabindex="-1">8. Paperclip fixes recovery ownership, completeness, Windows saves and PWA installability <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-paperclip-fixes-recovery-ownership-completeness-windows-saves-and-pwa-installability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;8. Paperclip fixes recovery ownership, completeness, Windows saves and PWA installability&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#17838 prevents startup recovery from stealing a heartbeat still preparing on the same server, avoiding a second executor against an incomplete workspace descriptor. #15063 retains the 100-level subtree limit but detects omitted eligible descendants and returns incomplete without stop fingerprints, review dispatch or mutations. #16925 serializes same-record ACPX saves within the process to prevent Windows EPERM rename races while allowing distinct records in parallel. #13461 requests standalone PWA installation rather than browser shortcuts; it does not add Push.</p><p>High learning value: ownership, complete scans and Windows concurrency address production failures. Medium-high competitive risk; retain the main radar and increase recovery/mobile-delivery attention without claiming a mature phone approval terminal. Pending lead: make proof completeness explicit. Interaction: incomplete versus stale evidence and cross-process coordination beyond an in-process queue.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17838" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/15063" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/16925" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/13461" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 4</a></p><h2 id="_9-eigent-uses-durable-request-identity-to-prevent-duplicate-automation-admission" tabindex="-1">9. Eigent uses durable request identity to prevent duplicate Automation admission <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-eigent-uses-durable-request-identity-to-prevent-duplicate-automation-admission" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Eigent uses durable request identity to prevent duplicate Automation admission&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; PACKAGED RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#2089 replaces random background task IDs with durable queue request IDs, allowing the admission transaction to mark and deduplicate the same request when its Session later opens. #2108 waits for failed-task reconciliation and settlement before draining follow-ups, backs off Session-busy retries from 1 to 30 seconds, and delays message publication/active selection until acceptance while retaining attachments. #2089 explicitly lacks a full scheduled Automation run in the packaged app; its tests are not desktop acceptance.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk; retain the main radar with closer admission-consistency monitoring. Pending lead: preserve one request identity across background, foreground and reconnect paths. Interaction: lost responses after execution without duplicate external effects.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent/pull/2089" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent/pull/2108" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_10-oacp-0-5-4-pins-trusted-scripts-by-content-hash" tabindex="-1">10. OACP 0.5.4 pins trusted scripts by content hash <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-oacp-0-5-4-pins-trusted-scripts-by-content-hash" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10. OACP 0.5.4 pins trusted scripts by content hash&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 0.5.4 introduces SHA-256 pins in envelope.trusted_scripts. pin-script re-signs receiver configuration and doctor detects missing/stale pins. Unpinned scripts and expansion-built program names ask for approval; compound command inspection is expanded and ask/deny decisions are logged. verify --attach-audit refuses another message&#39;s record, and finalization rejects uncovered realized grant-only effects.</p><p>High learning value, medium competitive risk; retain the protocol radar with more attention to script trust and audit binding. Pending lead: authorize concrete content and execution context rather than a pathname. Interaction: verification-to-execution TOCTOU and trust in changing script dependencies.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/kiloloop/oacp/releases/tag/v0.5.4" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_11-orca-1-4-242-enforces-ask-mode-and-preserves-resumed-permissions" tabindex="-1">11. Orca 1.4.242 enforces Ask mode and preserves resumed permissions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_11-orca-1-4-242-enforces-ask-mode-and-preserves-resumed-permissions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11. Orca 1.4.242 enforces Ask mode and preserves resumed permissions&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Versions 1.4.241/242 are released. In 1.4.242 Codex Ask runs read-only and asks before edits; permission changes apply to fresh turns while already-running work keeps its grant, and paired servers must update to change their old behavior. Resumed Claude retains effort/launch flags and Codex retains its last grant. Paired servers persist tabs across restarts and phones explain unreachable hosts. Sidebar Claude-subagent transcript navigation requires status hooks and fresh launches for existing sessions. Native Chat remains experimental.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk; keep it prominent in the second circle, emphasizing remote permission consistency. Pending lead: explain grants across phone/desktop/host versions and turns. Interaction: display, revoke and audit an older grant after a permission restriction.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/releases/tag/v1.4.242" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_12-uniemployee-0-23-0-packages-a-research-workbench-with-collection-and-migration" tabindex="-1">12. UniEmployee 0.23.0 packages a research workbench with collection and migration <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-uniemployee-0-23-0-packages-a-research-workbench-with-collection-and-migration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12. UniEmployee 0.23.0 packages a research workbench with collection and migration&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 0.23.0 releases a research workbench for embodied intelligence, AI frontier monitoring and policy analysis, with classification, provenance and versioned assessments. Its public-link pipeline includes tenant isolation, deduplication, private-address blocking, resumability and evidence-scope checks. Inbound research MCP is off by default and requires a random token of at least 32 bytes. Startup initializes tables/skills while preserving administrator personas, knowledge bases and user overrides. Passing tests are upstream release claims; no local installation was performed.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk: a vertical research product expansion, deserving greater second-circle attention. Pending lead: version and tenant boundaries across collection jobs, evidence and synthesis. Interaction: private-network protections through redirects/DNS rebinding and evidence scope after resume.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/zj-unicom-ai/UniEmployee/releases/tag/v0.23.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_13-agentlas-os-1-2-70-separates-native-delegation-from-execution-receipts" tabindex="-1">13. Agentlas OS 1.2.70 separates native delegation from execution receipts <a class="header-anchor" href="#_13-agentlas-os-1-2-70-separates-native-delegation-from-execution-receipts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13. Agentlas OS 1.2.70 separates native delegation from execution receipts&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 1.2.70 allows prepared agents to use current-host native delegation when available and authorized. Package read allowlists describe package contents; project/tool access remains governed by host permissions and explicit restrictions. Missing execution-receipt telemetry no longer blocks authorized work, while receipt validation stays strict and unvalidated work is not labeled validated execution.</p><p>High learning value, medium competitive risk; retain second-circle monitoring. Pending lead: separate authorization, execution, receipt arrival and verification. Interaction: late receipts, deadlines and effects on later admission when work is allowed to continue.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/agentlas-ai/Agentlas-OS/releases/tag/v1.2.70" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_14-superset-becomes-paradev-2-0-0-release-and-later-cloud-recovery-changes" tabindex="-1">14. Superset becomes Paradev: 2.0.0 release and later cloud recovery changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_14-superset-becomes-paradev-2-0-0-release-and-later-cloud-recovery-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;14. Superset becomes Paradev: 2.0.0 release and later cloud recovery changes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RENAME MERGED; DESKTOP 2.0.0 RELEASED; LATER FIXES MERGED</strong></p><p>#8874 merges the rename and publishing-path migration on October 26. The old superset-sh/superset URL redirects to ParadevAI/Paradev; both resolve to repository ID 1080370450, establishing identity. desktop-v2.0.0 is released. A paradev CLI alias is added while superset remains the actual binary. ELv2 has not become a permissive license; the license-file change relates to legal-entity naming. Later #8909 merges cloud boot/wake queuing, liveness gating and avoidance of ambiguous in-flight replay. #8795 TOML configuration remains a team-oriented dark launch, not general availability.</p><p>High learning value and high competitive risk; retain the main radar and record the verified rename mapping without replacing unrelated repositories. Pending lead: consistency of cold boot, resume, initial-prompt identity and outbox. Interaction: ambiguous in-flight messages, archive-hook failure and resource reclamation. The report concerns identity/delivery changes rather than funding publicity.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/8874" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/releases/tag/desktop-v2.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/8909" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/8795" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 4</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/blob/main/LICENSE.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 5</a></p><h2 id="_15-crewai-and-dspy-harden-checkpoint-interpolation-and-disk-cache-trust" tabindex="-1">15. CrewAI and DSPy harden checkpoint, interpolation and disk-cache trust <a class="header-anchor" href="#_15-crewai-and-dspy-harden-checkpoint-interpolation-and-disk-cache-trust" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;15. CrewAI and DSPy harden checkpoint, interpolation and disk-cache trust&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>CrewAI #8622 revalidates output_file after template interpolation and checks the resolved save path remains under cwd. #8623 fixes untrusted tool restoration from checkpoints, SQL-comment bypasses of read-only validation and tar-extraction races. DSPy #10726 enables restricted pickle loading by default: unregistered disk-cache types become cache misses, trusted directories may explicitly opt out, and memory caching is unchanged. These are merged changes with unverified release inclusion, not elimination of all deserialization risk.</p><p>Pending lead: construction-time validation does not authorize a later interpolated path; restored/cached data also needs a trust policy. Interaction: custom-type compatibility costs and remaining Windows-root, symlink and write-time races.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/pull/8622" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/pull/8623" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy/pull/10726" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_16-openhands-sdk-preserves-event-identity-and-replays-oauth-job-status" tabindex="-1">16. OpenHands SDK preserves event identity and replays OAuth job status <a class="header-anchor" href="#_16-openhands-sdk-preserves-event-identity-and-replays-oauth-job-status" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16. OpenHands SDK preserves event identity and replays OAuth job status&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#6155 prevents short secrets from corrupting UUID event/tool-call IDs during masking, which could break EventLog indexing and pause/resume reload. Typed ID fields and ID-only containers skip masking while surrounding text stays masked. In #5866 Engel Nyst directly contributes idempotent MCP OAuth callback behavior: completed jobs return stored status with 200; an unavailable callback server produces 409 only while the job remains unfinished.</p><p>Pending lead: preserve audit identity without breaking secret redaction, and consult durable state on repeated callbacks. Interaction: constrain string-ID formats so real secrets cannot be hidden in unmasked ID fields.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/pull/6155" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/pull/5866" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_17-ag2-checks-final-answers-and-documents-timeout-side-effects" tabindex="-1">17. AG2 checks final answers and documents timeout side effects <a class="header-anchor" href="#_17-ag2-checks-final-answers-and-documents-timeout-side-effects" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;17. AG2 checks final answers and documents timeout side effects&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#3506 corrects failure_attribution so intermediate text cannot hide a missing final answer or tool failure; it checks the last ModelResponse consistently with the evaluator projection. #3514 adds per-invocation function-tool timeouts covering dependency resolution, execution and cleanup, returning ToolErrorEvent on expiry; approval middleware is outside this deadline. Cancellation is cooperative, and a timed-out synchronous worker may continue producing side effects.</p><p>Pending lead: evaluation success, delivered answers, worker termination and cessation of effects are separate facts. Interaction: retry idempotency keys and attribution while a timed-out worker is still executing.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3506" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3514" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_18-langfuse-moves-self-hosted-fips-into-a-government-license-plan" tabindex="-1">18. Langfuse moves self-hosted FIPS into a Government license plan <a class="header-anchor" href="#_18-langfuse-moves-self-hosted-fips-into-a-government-license-plan" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;18. Langfuse moves self-hosted FIPS into a Government license plan&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#19316 merges self-hosted:gov and lf_gov_ keys with Enterprise entitlements and unconditional telemetry disablement. Breaking boundary: LANGFUSE_REQUIRE_FIPS=true requires a government key; existing enterprise keys would fail startup after upgrading to a version containing this change. Cloud is unchanged. The merge occurred after v4.70.1 was published, so this behavior is not attributed to that release.</p><p>This is a material upgrade/commercial boundary for evaluation/observability infrastructure. Pending CodeFlowMu/FCoP lead: license gates on compliance features can affect upgrade availability. Interaction: migration windows for enterprise FIPS users, offline-key verification and failure diagnostics.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/pull/19316" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v4.70.1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="cross-project-synthesis-and-disposition" tabindex="-1">Cross-project synthesis and disposition <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-project-synthesis-and-disposition" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-project synthesis and disposition&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Bind authorization to real actions:</strong> rewritten Codex arguments, OACP content pins and post-interpolation CrewAI paths show why static approval does not replace call-time checks.</li><li><strong>Recovery must not execute twice:</strong> Paperclip preparation ownership, Eigent request identity, Paradev outboxes, OAuth callbacks and file locks converge on this requirement.</li><li><strong>Preserve evidence boundaries:</strong> CatchBench proves consumption; AEGIS separates model refusal from gateway blocking; incomplete Paperclip scans do not prove stopping; Agentlas distinguishes execution from validated receipts.</li><li><strong>A wait timeout does not stop side effects:</strong> synchronous AG2 workers, CLI background jobs and subagent worktree lifetimes require separate treatment.</li></ul><p>Disposition: collection, classification and reporting only. Upstream experiments are attributed with limitations. Local engineering experiments: 0; articles: 0; development-review projects: 0; external comments/messages: 0. Research leads are not promoted directly into development reviews or assignments. Technical interaction topics are recorded in each item.</p><h2 id="dual-source-verification-coverage-and-gaps" tabindex="-1">Dual-source verification, coverage and gaps <a class="header-anchor" href="#dual-source-verification-coverage-and-gaps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dual-source verification, coverage and gaps&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Registry was read from latest main using the connected GitHub tool: 47 researchers, blob 8d97df65269aa2b4accd1366d1b71319f99c6efd. The six governance/verification researchers and relationship boundaries remain; no separate meaningful repository development was confirmed for them in this run. bulbasaur-skill-cli remains related work, not AGENTSAFE&#39;s official artifact. Yue Zhao is rendered as 赵越 in Chinese; people/repository attribution remains many-to-many.</p><p>Coverage spans 88 repository targets, including Registry engineering sources, explicit technical supplements and 32 competitor targets. GitHub checks cover releases, incremental commits and updated PRs/issues: 993 commit records; 48 zero-increment repositories received separate default-branch head reads. Superset&#39;s 301 was resolved through the web redirect, identical repository ID and official rename PR, retaining the original target and mapping to Paradev. Architecture, license and Discussion contents were not exhaustively reviewed for every repository; relevant primary text was inspected for selected developments.</p><p>Explicit gaps: Codex, Claude Code and Orca PR/issue feeds remain incomplete after 12 pages each and retain pending windows. TSA-AI-Workforce returns 404 on all three feeds; deletion/private status is not inferred. Codex&#39;s large release listing failed transport; latest three plus latest stable recovered bounded coverage only. Other release checks are limited to the latest ten, not all history. Failed, partial and no-change sources are distinguished; there is no global full-coverage watermark.</p><p>The second source type is reported separately: 12 grouped searches cover the current 47 researchers&#39; public social/author discussions, with direct opens of Cursor&#39;s official changelog, CatchBench PyPI, Paradev&#39;s official changelog and Audit Commons. No reliably dated in-window personal post was established; this does not exhaust X timelines. PyPI supports package availability. GitHub primary events independently support engineering facts; two GitHub links are not two source types. Cursor&#39;s currently visible October 20 Agent SDK entry is an out-of-window backfill lead, not today&#39;s launch. Audit Commons&#39; www URL was inaccessible; its bare domain was checked separately and recorded. Historical gaps remain explicit.</p><h2 id="github-archive-and-website-delivery" tabindex="-1">GitHub archive and website delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#github-archive-and-website-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GitHub archive and website delivery&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This report and its Chinese mirror contain the same 18 groups, states, sources and judgments. Only the two dated reports and existing index entries are updated, preserving styles and fixed entry points. Source publication uses the connected GitHub tool. Readback, latest-main/gh-pages concurrency checks, existing-site build, Pages publication and bilingual live click acceptance are recorded in a local receipt; this body does not pre-claim successful deployment. Public snapshots, original layout and other published content are preserved. Research Runtime business facts are untouched.</p>',103)])])}const g=a(i,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
